Perhaps I'm missing something, but as far as I can see, neither David
nor you have explained why some aspects of NH should be realistic (for
examples, see my earlier posts), while other aspects (such as pet
behavior) should not.  Since much of NH does, indeed, mirror RL, it
seems illogical at best to disallow proposed changes simply because
they, too, mirror real life.

Much of NH behaves the way it does because of history, literature,
and, yes, real life.  Take a look at a creature with .; most of them
will have some literary reference, some of which are real-life nature
books.  I think Dyllan has a page which lists the various sources
cited somewhere; it was a very impressive list.  Many of NH's official
features (such as tourists starting off with a credit card & camera)
are based on real life.  Or do you want to get rid of those as well,
simply because of where the basis for the idea came from?

I believe that my change makes pet behavior more logical.  Nothing in
my personal or literary experience has prepared me for so-called
"pets" actually being barely controlled, ravenning, bloodthirsty
beasts.  If you can justify this behavior, please do so.  I cannot.

